#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""The Samba Active Directory collector for Newton (SPEC-1.3 section 7).

The collector runs on the Samba domain controller as root, through
``sudo -n /usr/local/sbin/newton-ad-samba``, takes no input at all and
prints one JSON document on stdout.  It is the Samba half of the
collector pair: the Windows half is ``collector.ps1`` and the two produce
the same shape of document.

The collector runs only the query set it is installed with.  It never
takes a filter, a base or an attribute from anybody, and it reads the
directory through the Samba python bindings and never through
``ldbsearch``, because the LDIF that ``ldbsearch`` writes rewrites SIDs
and security descriptors as text.

The install is checked before anything else happens: the three files
of the install, the two directories that hold them and every parent of
those directories up to the root must be owned by root and must not be
writable by their group or by the others.  The check runs before the
samba bindings are imported, and the two files of the query set are
opened without following a link.

The SYSVOL is walked by the collector itself, never with ``os.walk``:
the base is opened without following a link, every folder is opened
relative to the descriptor of its parent and every file is opened
relative to the descriptor of its folder, so no path string is ever
opened below the base and a name that is swapped for a link between
the listing and the open is never followed.  A symlink and a special
file are never walked into and never read, and a file is opened without
following a link and without waiting for a writer.  The path that the
document carries is built from the fixed names of the spec, so that the
name of a file of the SYSVOL never reaches the document.

The identity of the document is the domain account ``newton-audit``:
its ``objectSid`` and its ``tokenGroups`` are read from the directory.
The account that logs in is the local account ``newton-ssh``, and
``SUDO_USER`` has to be exactly that one: anything else, or no
``SUDO_USER`` at all, is refused with exit 1.

Exit codes (SPEC-1.3 section 7):

* 0 when a document was produced, even when some queries failed;
* 2 on a forbidden-list refusal, an unsafe install or an argument;
* 1 when no document can be produced.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

